Checking `{[()]}(`:

| char | action | stack after |
|---|---|---|
| { | push | { |
| [ | push | { [ |
| ( | push | { [ ( |
| ) | top is ( ✓ pop | { [ |
| ] | top is [ ✓ pop | { |
| } | top is { ✓ pop | (empty) |
| ( | push | ( |

Every closer matched, but `(` is left open at the end → **not valid**.

```cpp
vector<char> st;
for (char c : s) {
    if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') st.push_back(c);
    else {
        char want = c == ')' ? '(' : c == ']' ? '[' : '{';
        if (st.empty() || st.back() != want) return false;
        st.pop_back();
    }
}
return st.empty();
```

Next greater elements of `[2, 1, 2, 4, 3]`:

| i | a[i] | popped (answer = a[i]) | stack of values after |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0 | 2 | — | 2 |
| 1 | 1 | — | 2 1 |
| 2 | 2 | 1 → 2 | 2 2 |
| 3 | 4 | 2 → 4, 2 → 4 | 4 |
| 4 | 3 | — | 4 3 |

4 and 3 are never popped: their answer stays −1. Result: `4 2 4 -1 -1`.

Histogram in C++:

```cpp
h.push_back(0);
vector<int> st;
long long best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)h.size(); i++) {
    while (!st.empty() && h[st.back()] >= h[i]) {
        int top = st.back(); st.pop_back();
        int left = st.empty() ? 0 : st.back() + 1;
        best = max(best, h[top] * (i - left));
    }
    st.push_back(i);
}
```

For `[2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 3]`, popping bar 5 (index 2) at i = 4 gives width 4 − 2 = 2 and area 10 — the answer.
